## Description

The custom build command artifact detection currently uses a hardcoded list of possible paths to find the built WebAssembly component. This approach is brittle and should be improved to handle more build patterns reliably.

## Location

`crates/wash/src/cli/component_build.rs:821` in the custom build artifact detection logic

## Current Behavior

```rust
// TODO(GFI): we need to handle this better.
// After running custom command, look for common build artifact locations
let possible_paths = [
    self.project_path.join("build").join("component.wasm"),
    self.project_path.join("dist").join("component.wasm"),
    self.project_path.join("out").join("component.wasm"),
    self.project_path.join("target").join("wasm32-wasip2")
        .join("release").join("component.wasm"),
    // ... more hardcoded paths
];
```

## Problems

- **Brittle detection**: Hardcoded paths don't cover all possible build configurations
- **No user feedback**: When artifacts aren't found, users don't know where the build looked
- **Limited flexibility**: Can't handle custom output paths or naming conventions

## Proposed Solution

1. **Configuration-based detection**: Allow users to specify artifact paths in config
2. **Smarter detection**: Use file modification times, patterns, or build tool conventions
3. **Better error reporting**: Show which paths were checked when artifacts aren't found

## Example Implementation

```rust
// Check config-specified path first
if let Some(artifact_path) = &self.config.output_path {
    if artifact_path.exists() {
        return Ok(artifact_path.clone());
    }
}

// Smart detection based on build tool patterns
let candidates = detect_build_artifacts(&self.project_path)?;

if candidates.is_empty() {
    bail!("No WebAssembly artifacts found. Checked paths: {:?}", checked_paths);
}

// Use most recently modified artifact
candidates.into_iter()
    .max_by_key(|path| path.metadata().unwrap().modified().unwrap())
    .unwrap()
```

## Benefits

- **More reliable**: Works with diverse build configurations
- **User-friendly**: Clear feedback when artifacts can't be found
- **Configurable**: Users can override detection logic when needed

## Description

Design and implement engaging empty states across the application that provide helpful guidance when there's no data to display. Empty states should be informative, visually appealing, and guide users toward appropriate actions.

## What to Create

- Empty state component library with various scenarios
- Illustrations and icons for different empty states
- Helpful messaging with clear call-to-action buttons
- Context-aware empty states for different sections
- Responsive design for all screen sizes

## Acceptance Criteria

- [ ] Empty states for all major data display areas
- [ ] Consistent visual design and messaging tone
- [ ] Clear call-to-action buttons where appropriate
- [ ] Responsive design for mobile and desktop
- [ ] Illustrations that match the application's design language
- [ ] Screen reader accessible content

## Technical Requirements

### Files to Create

1. **Empty State Component**
   - Path: `src/components/ui/empty-state.tsx`
   - Purpose: Reusable empty state component

2. **Empty State Variants**
   - Path: `src/components/ui/empty-state/`
   - Contents: Specialized empty state components

3. **Empty State Illustrations**
   - Path: `public/img/empty-states/`
   - Contents: SVG illustrations for different scenarios

4. **Empty State Hook**
   - Path: `src/hooks/useEmptyState.ts`
   - Purpose: Empty state logic and configuration

### Files to Modify

- `src/components/modules/marketplace/ui/pages/MarketplacePage.tsx`
- `src/components/modules/dashboard/ui/pages/DashboardPage.tsx`
- `src/components/modules/marketplace/ui/components/ActivityFeed.tsx`
- Any component that displays lists or collections

## Empty State Scenarios

### 1. No Pools Available
**Location**: Marketplace page when no pools exist
**Message**: "No lending pools available"
**Description**: "Be the first to create a lending pool and start earning interest on your assets."
**Action**: "Create Pool" button

### 2. No User Positions
**Location**: Dashboard when user has no positions
**Message**: "You haven't supplied or borrowed any assets yet"
**Description**: "Start your DeFi journey by supplying assets to earn interest or borrowing against your collateral."
**Actions**: "Supply Assets", "Browse Pools"

### 3. No Transaction History
**Location**: Activity feed when no transactions
**Message**: "No transaction history"
**Description**: "Your transaction history will appear here once you start interacting with pools."
**Action**: "Start Trading"

### 4. No Search Results
**Location**: Search/filter results
**Message**: "No pools match your criteria"
**Description**: "Try adjusting your filters or search terms to find more pools."
**Action**: "Clear Filters"

### 5. Wallet Not Connected
**Location**: Various pages requiring wallet
**Message**: "Connect your wallet to get started"
**Description**: "Connect your Stellar wallet to access lending pools and manage your positions."
**Action**: "Connect Wallet"

## Component Implementation

### Base Empty State Component

```typescript
interface EmptyStateProps {
  illustration?: React.ReactNode;
  title: string;
  description: string;
  action?: {
    label: string;
    onClick: () => void;
    variant?: 'primary' | 'secondary';
  };
  secondaryAction?: {
    label: string;
    onClick: () => void;
  };
  className?: string;
}

export function EmptyState({
  illustration,
  title,
  description,
  action,
  secondaryAction,
  className
}: EmptyStateProps) {
  return (
    <div className={cn(
      "flex flex-col items-center justify-center py-12 px-4 text-center",
      className
    )}>
      {illustration && (
        <div className="mb-6 opacity-60">
          {illustration}
        </div>
      )}

      <h3 className="text-lg font-semibold text-foreground mb-2">
        {title}
      </h3>

      <p className="text-muted-foreground mb-6 max-w-md">
        {description}
      </p>

      <div className="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-3">
        {action && (
          <Button
            onClick={action.onClick}
            variant={action.variant || 'primary'}
            size="lg"
          >
            {action.label}
          </Button>
        )}

        {secondaryAction && (
          <Button
            onClick={secondaryAction.onClick}
            variant="outline"
            size="lg"
          >
            {secondaryAction.label}
          </Button>
        )}
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}
```

